<h2>Background</h2>
For my System Dynamics final project, our team chose to model and control the pitch dynamics of a Boeing 747. We selected this topic because of our collective interest in aerospace engineering and its direct applicability to our future career paths. Aircraft pitch control is critically important: too much of a pitch angle can cause an aircraft to stall, while too little can send it into a dangerous dive. The goal was to design a feedback controller that maintains a desired pitch angle despite disturbances, ensuring both safety and passenger comfort.

<h2>Model</h2>
We began with the governing equations for an aircraft's pitch dynamics, which describe the rate of change of the angle of attack (α), pitch rate (q), and pitch angle (θ) as functions of the elevator angle (δ). These equations used Boeing 747 coefficients from a University of Michigan reference. From these state-space equations, we derived the transfer function from elevator angle input to pitch angle output:


### State-Space Representation

$$
\begin{bmatrix}
\dot{\alpha} \\
\dot{q} \\
\dot{\theta}
\end{bmatrix}
=
\begin{bmatrix}
-0.313 & 56.7 & 0 \\
-0.0139 & -0.426 & 0 \\
0 & 56.7 & 0
\end{bmatrix}
\begin{bmatrix}
\alpha \\
q \\
\theta
\end{bmatrix}
+
\begin{bmatrix}
0.232 \\
0.0203 \\
0
\end{bmatrix}
[\delta]
$$

$$
y =
\begin{bmatrix}
0 & 0 & 1
\end{bmatrix}
\begin{bmatrix}
\alpha \\
q \\
\theta
\end{bmatrix}
$$



### Transfer Function

$$
\frac{\theta(s)}{\Delta(s)} =
\frac{1.515s + 0.1774}{s^3 + 0.739s^2 + 0.921s}
$$

This transfer function formed the core of our control system design. Due to its format, we approximated initial PID gains of Kp = 1.515 and Ki = 0.1774, knowing these would be fine-tuned in simulation.

