We established the following design requirements based on passenger comfort and stall prevention:

- Maximum overshoot: less than 10% (to avoid stall conditions from excessive pitch angle)

- Rise time: between 0.1 and 2 seconds (fast enough for pilot response but not abrupt for passengers)

- Settling time: under 10 seconds

- Steady-state error: below 2%

If these requirements are not met, the aircraft could become uncontrollable, leading to stalls, dives, or other safety hazards.

I focused on creating the block diagram model that was later used in Simulink to implement a PID controller for pitch control. My task was to optimize the controller by adjusting the proportional, integral, and derivative gains to achieve minimal overshoot and steady-state error. Starting with our approximated values (K<sub>p</sub> = 1.515, K<sub>i</sub> = 0.1774, K<sub>d</sub>. = 0), we iteratively tuned the gains in Simulink until all design requirements were satisfied.


<h2>Performance</h2>

<p class="centered-image-block">
  <img src="{{ 'assets/images/5degreeplot.png' | relative_url }}"  width="400">
  <i>5 Degree Step Input Plot</i>
</p>


Our final tuned gains were:

K<sub>p</sub>. = 6 (proportional gain)

K<sub>i</sub>. = 4 (integral gain)

K<sub>d</sub>. = 5 (derivative gain)


With these gains and a 5-degree step input (simulating a pilot command), the system achieved:

- Overshoot: 3.17% (well under the 10% limit)

- Rise time: 288.7 ms (within the 0.1–2 second window)

- Settling time: 1.016 seconds (far under the 10-second requirement)

- Steady-state error: 0.8% (below the 2% limit)

We also tested a more aggressive 20-degree step input. The system responded with even better relative performance: 2.6% overshoot, 275.5 ms rise time, 1.019 s settling time, and only 0.1% steady-state error. This demonstrated that our PID controller was robust across a range of operating conditions.

We implemented the state-space model in MATLAB using Euler's method to simulate the angle of attack, pitch rate, and pitch angle over time. We also generated Bode plots for each output. Our analysis revealed important frequency-domain characteristics:

- Angle of attack: At low frequencies, α follows slow elevator deflections. Near the resonance frequency, the magnitude slopes downward, showing inability to respond to fast input changes. Phase starts near 0° but lags significantly at higher frequencies, settling around -90°.

- Pitch rate: Similarly follows slow changes but cannot react to fast fluctuations. Phase increases toward +45° near resonance, then drops and approaches -90°, representing consistent lag.

- Pitch angle: Starts with a -90° phase shift due to the integration effect of pitch rate, increases to -45°, then drops toward -180°, indicating a very delayed response to high-frequency inputs.

