<h2>Steady-State Analysis </h2>
One of the most insightful parts of our project was analyzing the eigenvalues and eigenvectors of the system matrix A. This analysis reveals the natural dynamic modes of the aircraft, how the aircraft inherently "wants" to behave without any control input. Understanding these modes is critical for steady-state analysis because it tells us whether the system is stable, how quickly it returns to equilibrium after a disturbance, and which combinations of states (angle of attack, pitch rate, and pitch angle) dominate the response.


We made a small, physically justifiable modification: adding -0.001 to the (3,3) entry since A was not invertible otherwise, representing minimal aerodynamic damping. This made A invertible without meaningfully changing the dynamics.

### Steady-State Gain (Alpha)

$$
\alpha := -C A^{-1} B + D
=
\begin{bmatrix} 1 & 0 & 0 \end{bmatrix}
\begin{bmatrix}
-0.313 & 56.7 & 0 \\
-0.0139 & -0.426 & 0 \\
0 & 56.7 & 0.001
\end{bmatrix}^{-1}
\begin{bmatrix}
0.232 \\
0.0203 \\
0
\end{bmatrix}
= [1.36]
$$



### Pitch Rate

$$
-CA^{-1}B + D
=
\begin{bmatrix} 0 & 1 & 0 \end{bmatrix}
\begin{bmatrix}
-0.313 & 56.7 & 0 \\
-0.0139 & -0.426 & 0 \\
0 & 56.7 & 0.001
\end{bmatrix}^{-1}
\begin{bmatrix}
0.232 \\
0.0203 \\
0
\end{bmatrix}
= [0.0034]
$$

Using the modified matrix, we computed the steady-state change in states due to elevator input:

Pitch rate steady-state gain: $K_q = -[0,\ 1,\ 0]A^{-1}B + D = 0.0034$

This means a 1-radian elevator deflection produces a steady pitch rate of 1.36 rad/s. The angle of attack showed even greater sensitivity, while pitch angle (due to the integrator) continues drifting without feedback.

The eigenvalue analysis directly guided our controller tuning:

- Stability confirmation: Negative real parts confirmed the open-loop aircraft is stable, so our PID only needed to improve performance, not stabilize an unstable system.

- Integrator: The near-zero eigenvalue explained why proportional control alone would leave steady-state error—we needed Ki > 0 to drive error to zero.

- Time constant estimation: Eigenvalues around -0.3 to -0.4 correspond to ~3-second time constants, meaning the system settles in about 10–15 seconds. This aligned with our 10-second settling requirement and guided our gain selection.

The eigenvalues revealed the fundamental limits of our system: no amount of control gain can change the inherent time scales set by the plant's natural modes. Understanding this trade-off between what can and cannot be changed through feedback is essential for any control engineer working on safety-critical systems.